  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The question often arises as to why heritabilities of dichotomous characters are measured on an unobservable underlying scale rather than on the directly observed scale. The latter approach was actually taken by most early investigators (Lush et al. 1948, Robertson and Lerner 1949, Dempster and Lerner 1950, VanVleck 1971). Assuming additivity, a simple expression for heritability on the observed scale can be obtained as follows. Suppose the character of interest is survivorship to a certain age. Then all nonsurvivors can be scored as 0 and all survivors as 1. The frequency of survivors in the population is simply the incidence $ \\Phi_{p} $, and the phenotypic variance is the familiar variance of a binomial distribution,",
      "source_page": 753,
      "printed_page": 733,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p015:b6",
        "Genetics_chapter25:p015:b7",
        "Genetics_chapter25:p016:b2"
      ],
