      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "b9b9f49e435ce5232d4dc8ad4507eba320685f3cc0d4e327916ee256dae1ad5b"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Examination of this formula reveals several undesirable properties of heritability estimates on the observed scale:",
      "source_page": 754,
      "printed_page": 734,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p016:b11"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        137.0,
        847.0,
        1094.0,
        913.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        754
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          137.0,
          847.0,
          1094.0,
          913.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "48216811621e292b043d45a268cc7d4996e53303fc1e921da3ec393ccbdb8c3b"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "1. $ h_{o}^{2} $ is a function of the incidence in the population (Figure 25.6). With constant phenotypic variance on the liability scale, $ h_{o}^{2} $ changes with a shift in mean liability because this induces a change in $ \\Phi_{p} $.",
      "source_page": 754,
      "printed_page": 734,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p016:b12"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        150.0,
        935.0,
        1063.0,
        1035.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        754
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          150.0,
          935.0,
          1063.0,
          1035.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "cdc833b7120f09377fc5b8427fd5c6f76a8a8a9eaf0dfc5587da02fc50c2d9da"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "2. The maximum possible value of $ h_o^2 $ is $ 2/\\pi \\simeq 0.64 $, which arises when $ h^2 = 1 $, $ \\Phi_p = 0.5 $, and $ p(x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-1/2} $. This implies that a substantial proportion of the genetic variance on the observed scale is nonadditive even if all of the genetic variance on the liability scale is additive. The reason for this relationship has been outlined in Figure 25.2 — the regression of risk on liability of genotypes is necessarily nonlinear since the probability of expressing the trait is bounded between 0 and 100%.",
      "source_page": 754,
      "printed_page": 734,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p016:b13"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        151.0,
        1044.0,
        1064.0,
        1272.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        754
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          151.0,
          1044.0,
          1064.0,
          1272.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "9f74b076324f7a412c820e4d19af2bd9e5c8bd36f18a81276e5098ea1d7304aa"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "3. For this same reason, genotypic values and environmental deviations are not independent on the observed scale.",
      "source_page": 754,
      "printed_page": 734,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p016:b14"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        152.0,
        1281.0,
        1064.0,
        1347.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        754
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          152.0,
          1281.0,
          1064.0,
          1347.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "7e6d11260be25f9d0ef4510d3e2b93460b9c8c3ac6465dd3d8592d7a1cdbfcd0"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Sometimes it is more practical to initially calculate heritability on the observed scale and then compute $ h^{2} $ indirectly by rearranging Equation 25.8b. Any of the procedures described in earlier chapters can be used for this purpose, applying the analysis to the 0, 1 variables. One problem with this approach, pointed",
      "source_page": 754,
      "printed_page": 734,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p016:b15"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        137.0,
        1377.0,
        1096.0,
        1510.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        754
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          137.0,
          1377.0,
          1096.0,
          1510.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "1787a3b05c3665cea28a23d119b0d45d743dfe3f7c715c39bc24d96e73c252a8"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FIGURE:25.6]]",
      "source_page": 755,
      "printed_page": 735,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p017:b2",
        "Genetics_chapter25:p017:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        110.0,
        644.0,
        970.0,
        708.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        755
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          248.0,
          138.0,
          838.0,
          611.0
        ],
        [
          110.0,
          644.0,
          970.0,
          708.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "5b074ef05a03058e061ae78a0dd96361370cb5ea953a5b6533ea1e0eac5fd118"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "out by VanVleck (1971), is that the relationship between $ h_{o}^{2} $ and $ h^{2} $ depends on the incidence $ \\Phi_{p} $ in a nonlinear fashion. Consequently, the substitution of sample estimates of $ \\Phi_{p} $ into Equation 25.8b results in a biased conversion between the two heritabilities.",
      "source_page": 755,
      "printed_page": 735,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter25:p017:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
